# --- part 1: turning points of the modern world
E+=[
X('magna-carta-1215','Magna Carta (1215): a failed peace treaty that became a legend of liberty',H,'confirmed',1,1215,['documentary','textual'],
 "On 15 June 1215, at Runnymede beside the River Thames, King John of England agreed to a charter drawn up to make peace with rebel barons. Its most quoted clause promised that no free man would be imprisoned or stripped of his rights except by the lawful judgement of his peers or by the law of the land. As a peace deal it failed within weeks: Pope Innocent III annulled it in August 1215 and civil war resumed. It was reissued in revised form in 1216, 1217 and 1225, and the 1217 reissue was accompanied by a separate Charter of the Forest. Four original copies of the 1215 charter survive.",
 [('A myth built later','In the 17th century the lawyer Sir Edward Coke presented Magna Carta as a guarantee of ancient English liberties in his struggles with the Stuart kings. Historians note that in 1215 it mainly protected the barons and the Church, and that much of its later meaning was given to it by later generations, including the founders of the United States.'),
  ('The forgotten companion','The Charter of the Forest of 1217 restored common people\'s rights to use royal forests for grazing, firewood and food. It stayed in force in England longer than any other statute, until 1971.')],
 ['wiki:Magna_Carta','wiki:Charter_of_the_Forest','wiki:King_John,_King_of_England'],img='Magna Carta',
 changed='1215: sealed and annulled. 1216-1225: reissued. 1600s: Coke recasts it as ancient liberty. 1791: echoes in the US Bill of Rights. 2015: 800th anniversary.'),
X('ninety-five-theses-1517','Luther\'s Ninety-five Theses (1517): did the hammer ever strike the door?',H,'confirmed',1,1517,['documentary','textual'],
 "On 31 October 1517 Martin Luther, a monk and professor at Wittenberg in Saxony, sent a list of 95 propositions to Archbishop Albert of Mainz, criticising the sale of indulgences, certificates said to reduce punishment for sins after death. The preacher Johann Tetzel was selling them to raise money, partly to rebuild St Peter's Basilica in Rome. Printers soon spread the theses across Germany, helped by the new printing press. Luther refused to recant at the Diet of Worms in 1521 and was excommunicated. The movement he started split Western Christianity into Catholic and Protestant churches and led to more than a century of religious wars.",
 [('The door that may not have happened','The famous image of Luther nailing the theses to the door of Wittenberg\'s Castle Church comes from an account by his colleague Philip Melanchthon written after Luther\'s death. In 1961 the Catholic historian Erwin Iserloh argued that the nailing never took place; others point to a note by Luther\'s secretary Georg Rörer that supports it. The question is still debated.'),
  ('Printing as the real hammer','Historians often argue that the Reformation succeeded where earlier reformers such as Jan Hus failed partly because pamphlets could now be printed in thousands of copies within weeks.')],
 ['wiki:Ninety-five_Theses','wiki:Martin_Luther','wiki:Reformation'],img='Ninety-five Theses',
 changed='1517: theses sent. 1521: Diet of Worms. 1546: Melanchthon describes the nailing. 1961: Iserloh disputes it. 2017: 500th anniversary.'),
X('magellan-elcano-1519','Magellan and Elcano: the first voyage around the world, and the day that went missing',H,'confirmed',1,1519,['documentary','textual'],
 "In September 1519 five ships with about 270 men left Spain under the Portuguese navigator Ferdinand Magellan, seeking a western route to the Spice Islands. They found the strait at the tip of South America now named after him and crossed the Pacific, an ocean far wider than anyone in Europe expected; many sailors died of scurvy and hunger. Magellan was killed at Mactan in the Philippines in April 1521. Juan Sebastián Elcano brought the last ship, the Victoria, back to Spain in September 1522 with 18 of the original crew, completing the first known circumnavigation of the Earth.",
 [('The lost day','When the survivors reached the Cape Verde Islands in July 1522, they found that their carefully kept ship\'s calendar was one day behind the local date. Antonio Pigafetta, the expedition\'s chronicler, recorded the surprise. By sailing west around the world they had experienced one fewer sunrise than people at home, the problem later handled by the International Date Line.'),
  ('Who went round first?','Magellan\'s enslaved interpreter, known as Enrique of Malacca, had been taken from Southeast Asia to Europe years before. If he made his way home after Magellan\'s death, he may have been the first person to travel all the way around the world. His fate is unknown, so this remains speculative.')],
 ['wiki:Magellan_expedition','wiki:Juan_Sebastián_Elcano','wiki:Antonio_Pigafetta','wiki:Enrique_of_Malacca'],img='Magellan expedition',end=1522),
X('french-revolution-1789','The French Revolution (1789): bread, rumours and a new calendar',H,'confirmed',1,1789,['documentary','textual'],
 "In 1789 a financial crisis forced King Louis XVI of France to call the Estates-General. Its commoner deputies declared themselves a National Assembly, and on 14 July a Paris crowd stormed the Bastille fortress, which held only seven prisoners that day. The Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen followed in August. The monarchy was abolished in 1792 and the king was executed in January 1793. During the Terror of 1793 to 1794 thousands were guillotined. The revolutionaries introduced the metric system and a new calendar with ten-day weeks, counting years from the founding of the Republic. Napoleon Bonaparte seized power in 1799.",
 [('The Great Fear','In the summer of 1789 rumours spread through the French countryside that bands of brigands paid by aristocrats were coming to destroy the harvest. No such bands existed, but peasants armed themselves and attacked manor houses. The historian Georges Lefebvre studied this "Great Fear" in 1932 as a classic case of a rumour panic.'),
  ('"Let them eat cake"','The line is popularly put in the mouth of Queen Marie Antoinette. Historians find no evidence she said it; a similar phrase appears in Jean-Jacques Rousseau\'s Confessions, attributed to "a great princess", written when Marie Antoinette was still a child in Austria.'),
  ('Rival readings','Historians long read the Revolution as a class struggle of the rising middle class; from the 1960s, revisionist historians such as François Furet stressed political ideas and culture instead.')],
 ['wiki:French_Revolution','wiki:Great_Fear','wiki:French_Republican_calendar','wiki:Let_them_eat_cake'],img='Storming of the Bastille',end=1799),
X('watt-steam-engine','Steam power and the Industrial Revolution, and the ancient engine that came first',H,'confirmed',2,1769,['documentary','technological'],
 "Steam engines were first used to pump water out of mines: Thomas Newcomen's engine began working in 1712. In 1769 the Scottish instrument maker James Watt patented a separate condenser that made steam engines far more efficient, and from 1775 his partnership with the industrialist Matthew Boulton sold them to mills and factories. Steam power, coal, iron and new textile machines drove the Industrial Revolution, which began in Britain in the later 18th century and spread worldwide. It changed how most people lived and worked, and began the large rise in carbon dioxide emissions from burning fossil fuels.",
 [('An engine 1,700 years early','In the 1st century CE Hero of Alexandria described the aeolipile, a sphere spun by jets of steam. Historians debate why it stayed a curiosity: suggestions include cheap slave labour, lack of suitable metalworking, and no pressing need such as flooded coal mines.'),
  ('The kettle legend','A popular story says young Watt got the idea from watching the lid of a boiling kettle. It was told after his fame and is usually treated as a legend.')],
 ['wiki:Watt_steam_engine','wiki:Industrial_Revolution','wiki:Newcomen_atmospheric_engine','wiki:Aeolipile'],img='Watt steam engine',
 changed='1st c. CE: aeolipile. 1712: Newcomen engine. 1769: Watt condenser. 1800s: railways, steamships. 20th c.: link to climate change recognised.'),
X('meiji-restoration-1868','The Meiji Restoration (1868): the samurai age ends, and Saigō is seen in Mars',H,'confirmed',2,1868,['documentary','textual'],
 "In 1853 US warships under Commodore Matthew Perry arrived in Japan and forced the Tokugawa shogunate to open the country to trade. In 1868 a coalition of domains overthrew the shogun and restored power to the young Emperor Meiji. The new government abolished the feudal domains and the samurai as a class, introduced conscription, built railways and factories, and sent students abroad to learn Western science. In 1877 the Satsuma Rebellion, led by the samurai leader Saigō Takamori, was crushed and Saigō died. Within a few decades Japan became an industrial power.",
 [('The star of Saigō','In 1877, while the rebellion was being crushed, the planet Mars was unusually bright, and popular prints showed Saigō\'s figure in it, calling it the "Saigō star".'),
  ('The hero who did not die','Rumours later spread that Saigō had survived and would return, one version saying he would come back from Russia. Such stories are found for many fallen heroes; the government pardoned him after his death and his statue stands in Tokyo\'s Ueno Park.')],
 ['wiki:Meiji_Restoration','wiki:Saigō_Takamori','wiki:Satsuma_Rebellion'],img='Meiji Restoration',end=1889),
X('einstein-1905','Einstein\'s miracle year (1905): a patent clerk rewrites physics',H,'confirmed',1,1905,['textual','experimental'],
 "In 1905 Albert Einstein, then 26 and working as a technical expert at the Swiss patent office in Bern, published four papers. One explained the photoelectric effect by proposing that light comes in packets (quanta), a foundation of quantum theory. One explained Brownian motion, giving strong evidence that atoms exist. One set out special relativity, in which time and space depend on the observer's motion. A short follow-up showed that mass and energy are equivalent, E = mc². His 1921 Nobel Prize was awarded for the photoelectric effect, not for relativity.",
 [('Who got there first?','Hendrik Lorentz and Henri Poincaré had developed much of the mathematics of relativity before 1905. Historians generally credit Einstein with the new physical interpretation, though the question of priority has been debated since.'),
  ('Mileva Marić','Einstein\'s first wife, the physicist Mileva Marić, studied with him. Some writers have argued she co-authored the 1905 work; most historians of science say the surviving evidence does not show this, while acknowledging her role is not fully known.')],
 ['wiki:Annus_mirabilis_papers','wiki:Albert_Einstein','wiki:Special_relativity','wiki:Mileva_Marić'],img='Annus mirabilis papers',
 changed='1905: four papers. 1915: general relativity. 1919: eclipse test. 1921: Nobel Prize for the photoelectric effect.'),
X('salt-march-1930','The Salt March (1930): a pinch of salt against an empire',H,'confirmed',2,1930,['documentary','photographic'],
 "Under British rule in India, making or selling salt without paying tax was illegal. On 12 March 1930 Mohandas Gandhi set out from his ashram near Ahmedabad with 78 followers and walked about 390 km to the coastal village of Dandi, arriving on 6 April. There he picked up a lump of natural salt, breaking the law. Mass civil disobedience followed across India and tens of thousands of people were arrested. The march drew worldwide attention to the independence movement, which succeeded in 1947.",
 [('Dharasana','In May 1930 nonviolent volunteers marched on the Dharasana salt works and were beaten by police with steel-tipped clubs. The American journalist Webb Miller\'s report was carried around the world and shaped opinion of British rule.'),
  ('An idea that travelled','Martin Luther King Jr. and other leaders of the US civil rights movement studied Gandhi\'s methods. Critics, including some Indian revolutionaries of the time, argued that nonviolence alone could not have ended British rule.')],
 ['wiki:Salt_March','wiki:Mahatma_Gandhi','wiki:Dharasana_Satyagraha'],img='Salt March'),
X('partition-of-india-1947','The Partition of India (1947): a border drawn in five weeks',H,'confirmed',1,1947,['documentary','oral history'],
 "When British rule ended in August 1947, British India was divided into two independent states, India and Pakistan (including what is now Bangladesh). The British lawyer Cyril Radcliffe, who had never been to India before, was given about five weeks to draw the borders through Punjab and Bengal. The boundaries were published on 17 August, two days after independence. Between 10 and 20 million people moved across the new borders, and estimates of those killed in communal violence range from several hundred thousand to about two million.",
 [('Who is to blame?','Historians debate the share of responsibility of the British government, which brought the date of withdrawal forward from June 1948 to August 1947, and of the leaders of the Indian National Congress and the Muslim League.'),
  ('Recovering memory','For decades much of the experience was not recorded. Since the 2000s oral history projects such as the 1947 Partition Archive have collected thousands of testimonies from survivors.')],
 ['wiki:Partition_of_India','wiki:Radcliffe_Line','wiki:1947_Partition_Archive'],img='Partition of India'),
X('berlin-wall-1989','The fall of the Berlin Wall (1989): history changed by a misread note',H,'confirmed',1,1989,['documentary','film'],
 "East Germany built the Berlin Wall in August 1961 to stop its citizens fleeing to the West; at least 140 people died at it. On the evening of 9 November 1989, amid mass protests, government spokesman Günter Schabowski announced new travel rules at a televised press conference. He had not been properly briefed. Asked when they took effect, he looked at his note and said, 'immediately, without delay'. Thousands of East Berliners went to the border crossings. At Bornholmer Straße the officer in charge, Harald Jäger, opened the gate that night. Germany was reunified on 3 October 1990, and the Soviet Union dissolved in December 1991.",
 [('An accident or a push?','Historians stress that the press-conference mistake only triggered a collapse already under way, driven by protests, refugees fleeing through Hungary and Soviet leader Mikhail Gorbachev\'s refusal to use force.')],
 ['wiki:Fall_of_the_Berlin_Wall','wiki:Günter_Schabowski','wiki:Berlin_Wall'],img='Fall of the Berlin Wall'),
X('end-of-apartheid-1994','The end of apartheid (1990-1994), and the "Mandela effect"',H,'confirmed',1,1990,['documentary','film','oral history'],
 "Nelson Mandela was released on 11 February 1990 after 27 years in prison, and the ban on the African National Congress and other movements was lifted. Negotiations between the government of F. W. de Klerk, the ANC and other parties followed, amid violence that killed thousands; the assassination of Chris Hani in April 1993 brought the country close to civil war. South Africa's first elections open to all races were held on 27 April 1994, now Freedom Day, and Mandela was inaugurated as president on 10 May 1994. A Truth and Reconciliation Commission heard testimony about crimes of the apartheid era.",
 [('The Mandela effect','In about 2009 the writer Fiona Broome found that many people remembered Mandela dying in prison in the 1980s, although he died in 2013. She named this the "Mandela effect", and some online writers took it as evidence of parallel realities. Psychologists explain such shared mistakes as false memories, which people can easily form and share.')],
 ['wiki:Negotiations_to_end_apartheid_in_South_Africa','wiki:Nelson_Mandela','wiki:Truth_and_Reconciliation_Commission_(South_Africa)','wiki:False_memory'],img='Nelson Mandela',end=1994),
]
